#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:18
msgid ""
"Nodes are the main units of computation in a ROS graph. This article "
"explains what nodes are, how they communicate, and how they provide "
"functionality to other parts of a ROS system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:35
msgid "Table of Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:38
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:40
msgid ""
"A node is a participant in the ROS graph that handles a specific task. The "
"behavior of nodes can be configured using parameters. Nodes advertise their "
"presence and establish connections with other compatible nodes through the "
"discovery process. Nodes communicate with each other using interfaces, like "
"topics, services, and actions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:46
msgid "About nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:48
msgid ""
"Nodes are the fundamental building blocks of a ROS system and represent "
"units of computation in a ROS graph. Each node is an independent participant "
"that handles one or more specific tasks, such as reading sensor data, "
"processing an algorithm, or driving a motor. Nodes can function as any "
"combination of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:52
msgid "A publisher to deliver data to other nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:53
msgid "A subscriber to get data from other nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:54
msgid ""
"A service client to have another node perform a computation on its behalf."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:55
msgid "A service server to provide functionality to other nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:56
msgid ""
"An action client to have another node perform a long-running computation on "
"its behalf."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:57
msgid "An action server to provide long-running functionality to other nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:59
msgid ""
"In a ROS system, there is typically a complex network of nodes as "
"publishers, subscribers, service servers, service clients, action servers, "
"and action clients, each acting with a different role simultaneously. This "
"network of nodes and the connections between them is known as the ROS graph. "
"See :doc:`How-ROS-Works`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:63
msgid ""
"Each client library provides a node type that you either create directly or, "
"in object-oriented languages such as C++ and Python, subclass to add your "
"own behavior. Configurable parameters enable you to control node behavior "
"during runtime. For example, a node controlling a motor may have a parameter "
"that controls the motor's maximum speed, while a topic might be used to set "
"a robot's current speed. See :doc:`parameters <About-Parameters>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:69
msgid "Communication between nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:71
msgid ""
"Nodes can communicate with other nodes within the same process, in a "
"different process, or on a different machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:73
msgid ""
"The ROS client libraries provide an API that allows you to write nodes in "
"multiple programming languages which can communicate with other nodes, even "
"if those nodes are not written in the same language. See :doc:`About-Client-"
"Libraries`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:76
msgid ""
"ROS nodes communicate through interfaces. See :doc:`Interfaces-Topics-"
"Services-Actions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:80
msgid "Discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:82
msgid ""
"Discovery of nodes happens automatically through the underlying middleware "
"of ROS. How discovery works depends on the :doc:`middleware (RMW) "
"implementation <client-libraries/About-Middleware-Implementations>` and how "
"it is configured. With the default DDS-based middleware, nodes find each "
"other through a distributed discovery process, with no central server. Other "
"configurations use server-client discovery instead, for example, a Fast DDS "
"discovery server, or the router that ``rmw_zenoh`` uses by default. "
"See :doc:`../Developer-Tools/Launch/Discovery-Server/Discovery-Server` "
"and :doc:`../Get-Started/Installation/RMW-Implementations/Non-DDS-"
"Implementations/Working-with-Zenoh`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:88
msgid "The distributed discovery process can be summarized as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:90
msgid ""
"When a node is started, it advertises its presence to other nodes on the "
"network with the same ROS domain (set with the ``ROS_DOMAIN_ID`` environment "
"variable). Nodes respond to this advertisement with information about "
"themselves so that the appropriate connections can be made and the nodes can "
"communicate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:92
msgid ""
"Nodes periodically advertise their presence so that connections can be made "
"with new-found entities, even after the initial discovery period."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:93
msgid ""
"Nodes advertise to other nodes when they shut down cleanly. If a node stops "
"unexpectedly, for example because it crashed, other nodes notice it is gone "
"when it stops sending its periodic advertisements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:96
msgid ""
"Nodes must share a ROS domain ID to discover each other. See :doc:`nodes/"
"About-Domain-ID`. You can also limit how far discovery reaches, for example "
"to the local host only, with ``ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE`` and "
"``ROS_STATIC_PEERS``. See :doc:`../Developer-Tools/Launch/Improved-Dynamic-"
"Discovery`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:102
msgid ""
"Discovering each other does not guarantee that two nodes exchange data. "
"Quality of service (QoS) compatibility is checked separately for each pair "
"of endpoints, for example, a publisher and a subscription on the same topic. "
"If their QoS settings are incompatible, that pair does not connect, even "
"though both nodes are visible in the ROS graph. See :doc:`interfaces/topics/"
"Working-with-topics/Quality-of-Service`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:107
msgid ""
"The :ref:`talker-listener demo <talker-listener>` provides an example of "
"node discovery. The C++ talker node in one terminal publishes messages on a "
"topic, and the Python listener node running in another terminal subscribes "
"to messages on the same topic. These nodes discover each other "
"automatically, and begin to exchange messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:112
msgid "Node management"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:114
msgid ""
"You can start individual nodes from the command line with ``ros2 run``. The "
"``ros2 run`` command initiates the executable binary which can launch one or "
"more nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:117
msgid ""
"ROS launch files allow you to start up and configure a number of executables "
"containing ROS nodes simultaneously. See :doc:`launch files <../Developer-"
"Tools/About-Launch>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:121
msgid "Log messages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:123
msgid ""
"Each node has an associated logger. By default, log messages go out to "
"targets including the console (on stderr), log files on disk, and the ``/"
"rosout`` topic. All of the targets can be individually enabled or disabled "
"on a per-node basis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:127
msgid "See :doc:`nodes/About-Logging/About-Logging`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:130
msgid "Special types of nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:132
msgid "Composable nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:133
msgid ""
"A composable node is written as a component which, at runtime, is loaded "
"into a container process that can host several components at once. "
"Components loaded into the same container run in a single process, and can "
"use intra-process communication to exchange messages without copying them. "
"To learn more about how to write a composable node, see :doc:`nodes/About-"
"Composition`, and :doc:`nodes/Working-with-nodes/Writing-a-Composable-Node`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:136
msgid "Lifecycle nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:137
msgid ""
"Lifecycle nodes can be used to ensure that resources are correctly "
"initialized, activated, deactivated, and cleaned up as the node moves "
"between lifecycle states."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:139
msgid ""
"A common use case is nodes that control hardware, where devices such as "
"cameras, lidars, motor drivers, and other sensors and actuators must be "
"started, configured, and shut down in a controlled order. See :doc:`nodes/"
"Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:141
msgid "Subordinate nodes (sub-nodes)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:142
msgid ""
"In C++ (``rclcpp``), a node can create secondary instances known as "
"**subordinate nodes** (or **sub-nodes**) using "
"``rclcpp::Node::create_sub_node()``. A subordinate node shares the parent "
"node's context, node interfaces (including logging, parameters, timers, and "
"clock), and ROS graph identity, while specifying an additional relative *sub-"
"namespace*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:145
msgid ""
"Subordinate nodes are useful for modular C++ software architecture. Internal "
"subsystems or helper libraries running inside the same node process can use "
"a subordinate node to publish or subscribe under scoped topic and service "
"names without creating separate nodes, avoiding additional middleware "
"discovery overhead and multi-node execution management."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/About-Nodes.rst:148
msgid ""
"For a practical guide, see :doc:`nodes/Working-with-nodes/Using-Sub-Nodes`."
msgstr ""

